Revision history for Selecto

Unreleased
    - MySQL/MariaDB date formats write years 1-999 with four digits, as
      PostgreSQL does (0001-02-03, not 1-02-03). A stored value PostgreSQL
      cannot hold (year 0, a zero month or day, an impossible date, or an
      instant shown past year 9999) is a typed query_error (sqlstate 22008,
      datetime_field_overflow) instead of an untyped failure. Format zones
      DateTime::TimeZone reads differently from PostgreSQL ('local',
      'floating', 'Z' and offsets such as '+0500') are refused with
      invalid_query.
    - Every MySQL/MariaDB read, stream and write checks its session first, in
      one round trip per call (a host can SET it at any time): a UTC time
      zone ('UTC', '+00:00', 'Etc/UTC', or SYSTEM only when the server's
      system zone is 'UTC' or '+00:00'; 'GMT' is refused, being also
      Europe/London's winter abbreviation), utf8mb4 client, connection and
      result character sets, a strict sql_mode, and a driver that returns
      utf8mb4 text and DATETIME values unchanged. Otherwise the call is
      refused with the new invalid_connection error (details.reason names
      the setting) before anything runs.
    - MySQL/MariaDB no longer infer an open host transaction from the
      server's @@in_transaction: the driver's AutoCommit alone decides
      between the write's own transaction and a savepoint. On MariaDB a write
      on a server transaction opened outside the driver (a raw START
      TRANSACTION with AutoCommit on) is refused with invalid_adapter
      (reason transaction_outside_driver) instead of running in a savepoint;
      MySQL cannot report one. Open host transactions with begin_work.
    - A MySQL/MariaDB upsert whose savepointed INSERT is the deadlock victim
      reports database_deadlock_detected (HTTP 503, retryable) again: the
      server had already rolled back the whole transaction, and the failed
      ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT replaced the typed error with an untyped one.
    - MySQL/MariaDB RETURNING re-reads are locking reads (FOR UPDATE). Under
      REPEATABLE READ a plain read returned the transaction's snapshot, so a
      delete's before image, or the row of a no-op update or upsert, could be
      older than the row the write locked.
    - MySQL and MariaDB compare tenant and site identity exactly, as
      PostgreSQL does (user decision 2026-10-14). Under the servers' default
      collations a text tenant column compared case- and accent-insensitively
      (and, under PAD SPACE collations, ignoring trailing spaces), so tenant
      't1' read and wrote rows of 'T1' and 't1 '. Every comparison of a text
      tenant, site or pinned column now adds a byte comparison,
      (col = ? AND CAST(col AS BINARY) = CAST(? AS BINARY)), with the value
      bound twice in text order: engine and required tenant predicates on the
      root, joined relations, members, retarget hops and rollup branches;
      tenant pairs and authored scope keys in joins, related collections and
      computed fields; member correlations on a tenant column; write
      predicates, RETURNING re-reads, guarded-insert admission and its stored
      row re-read, upsert target probes, graph children and reference guards.
      A caller's eq/IN filter on a tenant column compares exactly too.
      Numeric tenant columns, PostgreSQL, SQLite and the other dialects
      compile exactly as before.
    - MySQL and MariaDB upserts insert first inside a savepoint, so two
      sessions on one new key no longer deadlock on a shared gap lock; a
      duplicate on the declared target locks and updates its row, a duplicate
      on any other unique key is database_unique_violation. An existing row
      is found by a plain read first and locked directly, so racing updates
      of one row do not deadlock. Server errors are
      typed like PostgreSQL's (database_unique_violation,
      database_foreign_key_violation, database_not_null_violation,
      database_check_violation); a deadlock (1213) or lock-wait timeout (1205)
      is the retryable database_deadlock_detected / database_lock_timeout,
      answered with HTTP 503 by API::error_status. Upsert
      admission, guarded-insert admission and RETURNING key locking refuse a
      table that is not InnoDB (views and TEMPORARY tables, invisible in
      information_schema.TABLES, too); plain inserts and bulk updates run
      regardless. MariaDB LAG/LEAD with a default argument is refused.
      Related-collection keyset cursors no longer lose rows on MySQL/MariaDB
      (the null-safe rewrite swallowed an opening parenthesis).
    - A refused tenant-scoped MySQL/MariaDB upsert (unsupported_scope_predicate)
      is HTTP 501 from API::error_status in every runtime, not a 422.
    - A filtered association (one with where) is a lookup, not a reference:
      it picks one of the rows matching its owner key (a customer's option in
      one group) rather than the row the key identifies. It no longer counts
      as a declaration of its owner key's reference, so it neither contradicts
      that key's association or foreign key nor gets a write guard of its own.
    - A relationship whose child_key is the child's primary key and whose child
      domain is tenant-bounded (tenant_field or writes.scope.tenant) may name
      any parent_key under a tenant-bounded root: the parent holds its child's
      id (as a truck holds its trailer's), so it binds at most one child, which
      the child's tenant boundary keeps in reach. Without the child's tenant
      boundary it is still refused (foreign_key_tenant_scope_undeclared).
    - Encode JSON with Cpanel::JSON::XS (4.38 or later, now required) instead of
      JSON::PP. Domain and predicate fingerprints and Canonical JSON v1 bodies
      go through Selecto::CanonicalJSON and keep the bytes JSON::PP wrote,
      including whole numbers held as floats (3, not 3.0). Integer-looking
      floats of 1e16 and above, which JSON::PP wrote as strings, are now
      refused by canonical_json as floats.
    - Domain identity re-verification is opt-in (verify_domain_identity or
      $Selecto::Engine::VERIFY_DOMAIN_IDENTITY) and runs once per outermost call.
    - Add scalar engine with_tenant(value), write(), and action(name) as the
      common native facade. Domain-owned tenant columns and trusted context
      are inherited by reads, writes, actions, and transaction engines;
      omitted insert tenant values remain bound SQL data. Two-column native
      fluent writes preserve independent write scope through engine governance.
      Scalar engine with_tenant now binds trusted read/write scope: migrate
      read-only scalar attachment to with_read_context. Structured legacy
      with_tenant contexts stay read-only and cannot be promoted into writes.
      Existing scope/write_context constructors and Write->new stay supported.
    - Keep computed query fields, including derived action-prerequisite columns,
      out of write RETURNING all. Explicit computed projections refuse before
      execution instead of targeting nonexistent physical columns.
    - Support compound root primary keys in native domains, bounded writes,
      PostgreSQL edit handles and exact row-action targets. Every component
      is required and retained without JSON round-tripping. Encode Unicode
      query/action fingerprint inputs as UTF-8 before hashing.
    - Add native fluent write and domain-action helpers through
      Selecto::Write->new($engine), with trusted omitted-tenant fills,
      bound SQL previews, exact-count defaults and single-use execution.
      Existing immutable Operation builders remain compatible; upsert masks
      may narrow their assigned writable conflict fields.
    - Add engine transaction callbacks and PostgreSQL opaque editable-row
      results through return_for_update and select_for_update. Follow-up
      exact-key updates and owned one-to-many inserts share the caller's
      transaction; expired/forged references and unsupported result modes
      refuse. Caught child failures still roll back the callback. Errors
      after callback completion preserve unknown commit state and prohibit
      automatic retry. See docs/fluent-writes.md for capability limits.
    - MySQL and MariaDB support datetime_format as a selected value of the
      outermost query. The stored value is selected with a marker and
      formatted in Perl with DateTime::TimeZone (PostgreSQL gap/fold rules)
      because CONVERT_TZ leaves pre-1970 and (MariaDB) post-2038 values
      unconverted and drops DST after 2037. Formats in filters, grouping,
      ordering, nested sources, set operands or rollups, and streamed
      formatted statements, are refused with unsupported_feature.
      utc_datetime columns must hold UTC in DATETIME (a TIMESTAMP column is
      read through the session time zone).
    - MySQL and MariaDB writes: unscoped upserts follow PostgreSQL ON
      CONFLICT semantics without ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE (which resolves on
      the primary key before the declared target and has no WHERE). In the
      write transaction the declared target must be exactly a real unique
      index (under the table's metadata lock); the row holding the target
      values is locked (FOR UPDATE) and updated, or the row is inserted, so
      any other unique collision fails as in PostgreSQL. Upserts carrying a
      write scope (tenant, caller or required predicate) and bound graph
      child upserts are refused with unsupported_scope_predicate.
      RETURNING, generated keys and write graphs are emulated: update and
      delete lock the keys their full condition selects (FOR UPDATE), the
      write is confined to them, and rows are re-read by those keys and the
      tenant conjuncts only (or by the inserted key / conflict target);
      captured, written and re-read counts must agree or the write rolls
      back. Broad writes are bounded with locking key probes, guarded
      inserts are admitted against the column storage contract (strict
      sql_mode, no triggers, column type and collation) and the stored row
      is re-checked against the enforced predicate before commit. Updates
      and upserts refuse a handle whose DSN disables
      mariadb_client_found_rows. Query budgets set max_execution_time /
      max_statement_time as literals (DBD::MariaDB quotes bound values).
    - MySQL and MariaDB adapters gain the advanced read features, each gated
      on the connected server version: CTEs and recursive CTEs, window
      functions (rows and range frames), INTERSECT/EXCEPT, rollups (as
      grouped UNION ALL branches with typed NULL dimensions, since MariaDB
      has no GROUPING() and neither server has NULLS FIRST/LAST), LATERAL
      joins (native on MySQL 8.0.14+; on MariaDB lowered to a derived table
      with per-parent ROW_NUMBER, refusing grouped or aggregate children),
      JSON_TABLE JSON and array rowsets, exact
      jsonb-style JSON containment and typed array predicates (JSON_CONTAINS
      also matches scalars inside nested arrays, so it is not used), JSON
      text extraction, value expressions, ordered and per-parent bounded
      related collections, and FULLTEXT boolean-mode text search for
      co-domain lookups. The SQLite rollup emulation moved to
      Selecto::SQL::RollupEmulation, shared with them.
    - MySQL and MariaDB results order NULLs as PostgreSQL does: last
      ascending and first descending. Every ORDER BY term (queries, set
      operations, windows, rollups, related collections) gets a leading
      (expression IS NULL) key, and related-collection cursors compare with
      <=> so keyset pages stay consistent with that order. Previously both
      servers sorted NULLs first ascending.
    - SQLite batches may combine protected UPSERTs with ordinary inserts,
      updates, deletes and upserts (and protected inserts) when each
      protected candidate is proved independent of every other command on
      its table, ported from the Elixir reference. The proof compares
      arbiter keys, stored and final candidate rows, other UPSERTs' own
      arbiters (the different-unique-key hazard), UPDATE/DELETE predicates
      and key-moving updates through SQLite affinity and collation, in
      both orders and both directions between protected candidates. Unknown
      generated rowids, volatile defaults, same-table cleanup and anything
      else unprovable refuse as required_data_rule_pending before DML.
      Previously every such batch refused; stored UPDATE/DELETE deltas, a
      protected insert beside an ordinary delta, and protected UPSERTs in
      graphs still do.
    - Document and pin that on a native DBD::SQLite handle every write
      governed by stored-stage rules (insert, update and upsert, since the
      protected UPSERT port) requires an open native transaction on the
      adapter's connection. A host transaction_handler that runs the work
      in autocommit, contrary to its documented contract, is refused with
      required_data_rule_pending (native_transaction_required) before DML;
      before the port such inserts and updates ran their candidate proof
      and write non-atomically. Writes without stored rules and handlers
      that open a transaction are unaffected.
    - Protected SQLite UPSERT receipts are rechecked against the live
      trusted write context at dispatch. The engine now opens the candidate
      scope with its write context; projection refuses a request under a
      different context and the dispatch recheck compares the receipt with
      the running scope's context (a nested scope swaps it in and restores
      it), refusing with required_data_rule_pending, reason
      receipt_context_mismatch. Previously dispatch compared the receipt's
      recorded context with itself, contrary to the docs.
    - An empty UPSERT update mask compiles to DO NOTHING only on the
      protected SQLite path (a SQLite engine whose domain has stored UPSERT
      rules, dispatched with its private candidate receipt). Since the
      protected UPSERT port every adapter accepted an empty mask; PostgreSQL,
      DuckDB, MSSQL and unprotected SQLite engines refuse it again with
      invalid_write, and their upsert actions with no updatable change with
      invalid_action_plan. The HTTP API still requires a non-empty mask.
    - SECURITY: a portable-compiled protected UPSERT, whose choice
      membership is deferred to native branch projection, is refused with
      compiled_upsert_mismatch when the executing engine's domain, trusted
      context or stored UPSERT rules differ from the compiler's. Previously
      an engine with no stored UPSERT rules returned before governance, so
      the deferred membership was never checked (reproduced: a resolver
      that rejected "forbidden" was never called and the row was stored
      through execute_write, preview_write and execute_batch). The check
      runs in governed_write, before any provider or DML.
    - SECURITY: INPUT and ACTION_INPUT bindings whose rule or condition
      reads another root path (value.compare_path) are re-tested, without
      normalizers, against the fully normalized submission. Bindings run in
      sorted-id order, so a confirmation binding could pass against a
      related field before that field's own normalizer ran (reproduced:
      confirm "A@X" passed eq ['email'] and email was stored as "a@x").
      The recheck covers native writes, previews, portable compilation and
      action plans, and refuses with data_rule_failed before DML. Bindings
      reading only their own path keep their ordered semantics.
    - SECURITY: a protected SQLite UPSERT refuses a connection with
      PRAGMA read_uncommitted on (required_data_rule_pending, reason
      read_uncommitted_isolation). On a shared cache such a connection
      takes no table read locks: in a deferred transaction (caller-owned
      BEGIN DEFERRED, or adapter-owned with
      sqlite_use_immediate_transaction off) the candidate proof could read
      a peer's uncommitted conflict row, validate the conflict branch
      against it, and then, after the peer rolled back between the
      dispatch recheck and the business DML, insert a row whose rules were
      never checked (reproduced: a nonblank-extra rule admitted a NULL
      extra). A peer committing in the same window turned a proved insert
      into an unproved update. The scope, each projection and the dispatch
      recheck read the pragma; the caller's transaction, savepoints and
      pragma are left unchanged. A private connection with the pragma on
      refuses too, as the adapter cannot tell a shared cache apart.
      Default locking reads, shared or not, are unaffected.
    - SECURITY, BREAKING: on a two-column domain under an engine tenant,
      every write that sets site_id needs a host or required site pin
      (lesson 47b). Once writes bind tenant_id only (47a), tenant 7 could
      relabel its own row to tenant 8's site 7 and then read tenant 8's
      site 7 rows through by-name site pairing (41c); at 47a this worked
      through Engine writes, the public write route, batches, graph roots
      and children. Now an insert, update, upsert, batch member, graph
      root or child, owned-sync row, API write or action that sets site_id
      is refused with missing_tenant_scope unless a positive top-level
      eq/IN conjunct of the required predicate or a trusted write_context
      required filter pins the allowed sites (host and required pins count
      alike and are intersected; a pin set they leave empty is
      tenant_mismatch); the value must be a literal inside the pin
      (tenant_mismatch), also on update. An insert omitting site_id is
      assigned the pin when exactly one site is pinned and refused
      otherwise. A graph child tenanted on site_id is checked against the
      root's pin intersected with its own, is never given site_id = engine
      value, and its updates, deletes and owned-sync cleanup match only
      rows inside a pin. Writes that do not set site_id are unaffected.
      Previews refuse exactly what execution refuses. Host-visible: such
      inserts now require a site pin.
    - BEHAVIOUR, BREAKING: on a two-column domain (source.tenant_field
      tenant_id, writes.scope.tenant site_id) the engine tenant means
      tenant_id on writes too, as on reads (lesson 47a, reversing the
      write half of 41a that 41c kept). Before, every write bound
      site_id = engine value: with tenant 7 at sites 70 and 71 and tenant 8
      at site 7, an engine tenant 7 update, delete, API write or graph root
      changed tenant 8's site 7 row and could not reach tenant 7's own
      rows, inserts were assigned site_id = 7, and a graph child tenanted
      on site_id was bound site_id = 7. Now writes bind tenant_id = engine
      tenant (lesson 47's rules), and site_id is never bound to the engine
      value: a host trusted-scope (write_context required_filters) or
      required predicate conjunct on site_id pins it, and a caller filter
      on it only narrows. A writes.scope.tenant on site_id still requires
      a trusted tenant. Reference guards pairing with site_id take the
      pinned set (refused with missing_tenant_scope without one), stored
      child candidates of data rules are read under the same binding, and
      the portable foreign-key guard description pairs the context tenant
      with tenant_id. Host-visible: an engine tenant's writes on such a
      domain now touch the rows of that tenant_id at any site.
    - SECURITY, BREAKING: a trusted engine tenant limits every write of a
      domain that declares source.tenant_field, with or without
      writes.scope.tenant (lesson 47). Before, such a domain without
      writes.scope.tenant was written unbounded: under engine tenant 7 an
      update or delete with a required or host (write_context
      required_filters) tenant_id = 8, a tenant_id IN (7, 8), or only a
      caller filter id = 2 changed tenant 8's rows, through Engine writes,
      the public API write route and graph roots. Now updates, deletes,
      upserts, batch members, graph roots and children, owned-sync
      cleanup, actions and query-enforced writes AND tenant_id = 7 into
      their scope; inserts and upserts carry it (filled when absent,
      tenant_mismatch when another value is given or an update re-tags a
      row); an upsert must resolve conflicts on a target including
      tenant_id (tenant_scope_conflict_target). A required, host, caller
      or query-evidence predicate whose top-level tenant_id conjunct
      excludes the engine tenant is refused with tenant_mismatch before
      any DML, also on a domain whose writes.scope.tenant names that column
      (it matched no row before); an IN list admitting it is intersected.
      Host-visible: the public write handler accepts an engine tenant as
      the write boundary of such a domain (it was missing_tenant_scope),
      and an engine-tenant write that used to reach other tenants' rows
      now affects only its own. Every update of a root tenant column,
      the writes.scope.tenant column included, must keep a value its
      required and host conjuncts admit. A graph child binds the engine
      tenant only onto a column pairing with the root's. A domain whose
      writes.scope.tenant names the tenant column is otherwise unchanged,
      and a public write with no tenant boundary is still refused with
      missing_tenant_scope.
    - SECURITY: a plain join path past an untenanted hop keeps the root's
      admitted tenant set (lesson 41f). root (tenant_id) -> shared bin ->
      tenant notes, with the root bounded only by a required predicate
      (tenant_id IN (7, 8) or = 7, no engine tenant), paired tenants per
      adjacent edge only, so the notes went unbounded: bin.notes.body,
      a through join's labels, a filter on bin.notes.body, the public
      query handler and API route (200), and a retarget to the bin
      returned tenant 9's notes. The lesson 41b change binds such a
      relation to the root's admitted set (IN (7, 8)) as well as to any
      engine tenant, and refuses it with missing_tenant_scope when the
      root is bounded by a predicate without a tenant set; this pins the
      join, through-join, filter, handler, API and retarget forms. A root
      with no boundary still reads every tenant.
    - SECURITY: on a two-column root a shared hop that merely stores the
      write-scope column is not a by-name pairing source (lesson 41e).
      customers (tenant 7, site 5) -> shared bins (a bin at site 7) ->
      lines tenanted on site_id returned tenant 8's site 7 line through the
      join path (Engine reads and the public query handler), a filter on
      bin.lines.code, retarget('bin.lines'), retarget('bin') plus
      lines.code or a lines filter, and a collection after
      retarget('bin'): under an engine tenant the line was bound
      lines.site_id = 7 (the engine value), and under a required tenant_id
      predicate alone it was unbounded, also when the shared hop authored
      a site_id scope pair. A site_id relation now pairs only with a hop
      chained back to the root's tenant-bound rows, or is refused with
      missing_tenant_scope (the lesson 41b change); this pins every access
      form. Site rows paired with the root itself are unchanged.
    - SECURITY: every hop of a two-column domain pairs tenant columns by
      name, and an untenanted retarget hop carries the root's admitted
      tenant set (lesson 41b). On a two-column root (source.tenant_field
      tenant_id, writes.scope.tenant site_id) only root hops paired by
      name: nested hops and the domain a retarget rebuilds at its target
      lost the root's write scope and paired positionally, so once reads
      bind tenant_id (41c) an accounts hop paired accounts.tenant_id =
      lines.site_id (a tenant 7 read of account.lines.code, or of
      retarget('account') + lines.code, returned site 7's line) and a lines
      hop paired lines.site_id = notes.tenant_id (lines.notes.body
      returned tenant 5's note at site 5). Every hop
      now pairs a tenant column only with a same-named tenant column of a
      hop chained to the root; a tenant_id relation reached otherwise is
      bound to the engine tenant or the root's admitted set, and a site_id
      relation reached otherwise is refused with missing_tenant_scope. A
      retarget target carries each root conjunct only onto its same-named
      column; a site_id target chained to the root by name is admitted on
      the retarget context's bound. And under a required predicate such
      as tenant_id IN (7, 8) with no engine tenant, a retarget to a shared
      hop (retarget('bin')) let later tenant joins, filters, collections
      and inline sources read every tenant's notes; the rebuilt domain now
      carries the root's admitted set onto them (as IN), and refuses them
      with missing_tenant_scope when the root's boundary has no tenant set.
    - SECURITY: every Engine read honours the trusted engine tenant
      (lesson 43). Probed under engine tenant 7 with tenant 8 rows present,
      on SQLite and PostgreSQL: all, compile, stream, projection_sum,
      export scalars, BoundedQuery, canned pages, the public query handler,
      joined fields and filters, retargets (IN and EXISTS), related
      collections, query members, inline subqueries and laterals, lateral
      lookups, query library segments and with_limits copies all bound the
      root of a tenant_field domain to the engine tenant already, and a
      required predicate agreeing with it or wider (tenant_id IN (7, 8))
      returned only tenant 7's rows. A required tenant condition that
      excludes the engine tenant (required_filters, a required predicate
      or require_tenant_filter) was refused only as tenant_scope_ambiguous;
      it is now refused with tenant_mismatch before any read path runs,
      and require_tenant_filter refuses it when it is attached.
    - BEHAVIOUR: on a two-column root (source.tenant_field tenant_id,
      writes.scope.tenant site_id) the engine tenant bounds reads on the
      tenant_field column only (lesson 41c, reversing that part of 41a).
      The root binds tenant_id to the engine tenant, a relation whose tenant
      column is tenant_id pairs s0.tenant_id and so carries that bound, and
      a relation whose tenant column is site_id is never bound to the
      engine value: it pairs s0.site_id by name with the tenant-bound root
      rows, returning the caller's own site rows. Before (since 41a) a
      site 7 engine read bound site_id = 7 on reads and ignored tenant_id,
      so a tenant 7 engine read returned another tenant's rows whose
      site_id was 7. A required predicate on tenant_id is intersected with
      the engine tenant, and one excluding it is refused. A query member,
      inline source or retarget target over a site_id relation carries
      only a site_id bound of the root's required predicate, so with none
      it is refused with missing_tenant_scope (also when the domain is
      parsed: the write scope is now known before members are validated,
      where it had paired positionally). By-name pairing and the
      invalid_domain refusal of a target tenant column matching neither
      root column are kept. Writes still bound the write-scope column
      until lesson 47a, which binds tenant_id on writes too.
    - SECURITY: the engine tenant survives domain rebuilds (lesson 42a).
      Multi-hop joins and retargets from a shared root through a shared
      middle hop (products -> categories -> notes), engine copies and
      parsed-domain rebuilds were already bounded and are now pinned by
      tests. A directly constructed domain whose association records its
      target's tenant field lost it through as_contract, so the rebuilt
      domain joined every tenant's rows; the portable projection now keeps
      the target schema's tenant_field.
    - SECURITY: on a two-column root (writes.scope.tenant on one column,
      source.tenant_field on another) reads pair tenant columns by name,
      never by position (lesson 41a). A join to a target whose tenant_field
      names the write-scope column (site_id) inferred
      s0.tenant_id = target.site_id and the lesson 41 pairing repeated it;
      it now pairs s0.site_id = target.site_id, and a target tenant column
      matching neither root column is refused with invalid_domain (details
      code foreign_key_tenant_scope_undeclared). The engine tenant bounds
      only the write-scope column on reads too, as on writes: before, a
      read bound tenant_field to the engine tenant, so a site 7 engine read
      the rows whose tenant_id was 7 (or refused with
      tenant_scope_ambiguous beside a required tenant_id predicate). Query
      members, inline sources and retarget targets carry the root column
      of their own tenant column's name.
    - SECURITY: every query source over a tenant-scoped relation carries the
      root's trusted tenant boundary or is refused with missing_tenant_scope
      (lesson 39). Inline sources built with with_cte, with_recursive_cte,
      subquery_join and lateral_join over a domain declaring tenant_field
      ran unbounded under a root bounded by the engine tenant or only by its
      required predicate (required_filters or with_required_predicate): a
      tenant 7 read summed tenant 8's orders through an inline CTE, subquery
      or lateral. They now receive the root's tenant conditions re-expressed
      on their tenant field, as declared query members already did, and a
      scoped root whose predicate has no tenant condition refuses them. A
      root relation without a tenant field of its own now applies the
      trusted engine tenant to every tenant-scoped declared member, inline
      source and retarget target; before, those read every tenant's rows.
      Sources already bounded by a same-tenant configured engine are
      unchanged, and roots with no boundary keep reading every tenant.
    - SECURITY: under a trusted engine tenant, a read from a shared root
      (one that declares no tenant_field) binds every joined or through
      relation that declares tenant_field to the engine tenant, in the join
      condition, so a left join keeps the shared row without another
      tenant's (lesson 42). Before, the engine tenant bounded only domains
      that themselves declare tenant_field, so a products root under tenant
      7 returned tenant 8's orders.total through Engine reads, lateral
      lookups and the public query route (200), and such a field was a
      filter oracle. A tenant-scoped parent keeps carrying the bound through
      its tenant pair (lesson 41).
    - SECURITY: a join between two relations that both declare tenant_field
      pairs their tenant fields even when the association authors scope
      keys on another column (lesson 41). Before, an authored pair such as
      region_id -> region_id replaced the inferred tenant pair, and through
      associations never paired tenants, so under a root bounded by the
      engine tenant or only by its required predicate a tenant 7 read of
      `select id, account.name` (or a through association's field) returned
      tenant 8's row sharing tenant 7's region or bridge row, through Engine
      reads, lateral lookups and the public query route (200), and such a
      field was a filter oracle. Authored pairs are kept as well. A target
      whose declared tenant_field is not one of its fields is refused with
      invalid_domain (details code foreign_key_tenant_scope_undeclared).
    - SECURITY (low): a choice source or co-domain declaration that would
      return a redacted (or internal or hidden) field is refused when the
      engine is built (lesson 37), with invalid_domain and details code
      choice_source_redacted_field (a choice source's value_source,
      caption_source or description_source, a field reference's
      value_source, caption_source or caption_field, or value/label fields of
      a choice domain this contract describes) or co_domain_redacted_field (a
      co-domain targeting this domain). Before, a host resolver was handed a
      choice source naming a redacted field such as owners.ssn as its
      caption or description. Co-domain lookups into another domain already
      refused its non-public fields at lookup time (invalid_co_domain); that
      is now pinned by a test.
    - SECURITY: context-sourced foreign keys (source ['context', k]) are
      paired like input foreign keys (item 34). A foreign key that records
      no tenancy of its own no longer drops the tenancy an association on
      the same owner key records: before, its context-owned existence-only
      exemption let a context person_id reference another tenant's person
      although the association paired person.tenant_id with the root's
      tenant_id. The exemption now applies only when nothing declares the
      target's tenancy. On a two-column root the guard takes the set of the
      root column it pairs with (lesson 29e), so the site value never guards
      a tenant_id column, and the portable description from
      Selecto::Write::Context no longer pairs the trusted context tenant
      (the write-scope column's) with another tenant column.
    - SECURITY: graph children and owned-sync rows are confined to the root's
      admitted tenants exactly as the root is (lesson 33). Under a root
      bounded only by its required predicate (no engine tenant), a child
      that stores the root's tenant column could be inserted or re-tagged
      with another tenant's tenant_id, update or delete a tenant 8 row
      under tenant 7's parent, and owned-sync cleanup deleted such rows.
      Now, unless the child declares its own writes.scope.tenant, each root
      tenant column it stores is ANDed into its UPDATE and DELETE scope (and
      into owned-sync cleanup), an insert must land inside the set (the
      adapter admits insert candidates against the scope), an assignment to
      the column must be one of the set's literal values (tenant_mismatch,
      before any DML), and an upsert is refused
      (query_enforcement_unsupported_operation), as on the root.
    - SECURITY: under a tenant-bounded root (an engine tenant, a
      tenant_field, writes.scope.tenant or a required predicate) a graph or
      sync relationship's parent_key must be the parent's primary key or
      exactly a single-column upsert conflict target the parent declares
      (lesson 36), at every hop of nested relationship domains. Otherwise
      the engine is refused when it is built with invalid_domain (details
      code foreign_key_tenant_scope_undeclared, relationship, parent_key).
      A composite conflict target or a sync identity does not count.
      Previously, on PostgreSQL, parts bound to their item by a code two
      tenants' items shared were reachable through tenant 7's item: a
      two-hop graph updated tenant 8's part. (SQLite already refused it
      through its native ownership storage proof.)
    - SECURITY: on a two-column root (writes.scope.tenant on one column,
      source.tenant_field on another) reference guards took the write-scope
      tenant set whatever root column they paired with. An association
      authored as source_scope_key tenant_id -> target_scope_key site_id was
      guarded as warehouses.site_id IN <site set>, accepting another
      tenant's row and refusing the right one; a target column named after
      no root column (tenant_id -> org_id) accepted a row tagged with the
      site value. Each root column now has its own admitted set (the engine
      tenant for the write-scope column, the required predicate's eq/in
      conjuncts for the tenant_field column) and a guard takes the set of
      the column it pairs with (lesson 29e): an authored pair takes its
      source_scope_key's set, a target storing both root columns is guarded
      on each by name with its own set, and any other tenant column pairs
      by name. Scope keys that disagree with the target's tenant_field, a
      column two pairings would give different sets (29j), an authored pair
      crossing names on a both-columns target, and a tenant column pairing
      with no root column are refused with invalid_domain
      (foreign_key_tenant_scope_undeclared) when the engine is built; a
      reference whose paired column no set bounds fails with
      missing_tenant_scope.
    - SECURITY/correctness (PostgreSQL): read guards (bounded streams,
      savepoint query budgets, direct bounded reads) and managed writes no
      longer start on a connection whose transaction they cannot account
      for. A pending asynchronous host result (pg_async before pg_result),
      a busy connection, an already aborted host transaction or an unknown
      state is refused with the new typed error transaction_state_unsafe
      (details state: active, inerror or unknown) before any statement is
      sent. Previously, with a pending async INSERT inside a raw BEGIN, a
      bounded stream or managed write treated the connection as idle and its
      rollback discarded the host's transaction (its earlier INSERT and the
      pending one), so the host's next INSERT autocommitted; inside an
      aborted transaction a savepoint budget failed with
      query_budget_unavailable and poisoned the handle for later budgets.
    - SECURITY/correctness (PostgreSQL): a bounded stream rolls back only
      what it can prove is its own. When the host aborted the transaction
      while a stream was open inside it (a failing statement, or a failing
      async query), the stream's next FETCH or close rolled back to its
      savepoint and so silently un-aborted the host's transaction: the
      host's later INSERT and COMMIT went through. When the host took the
      connection over during a stream in its own transaction (ROLLBACK,
      BEGIN, INSERT), the stream's close rolled back the host's new
      transaction. The stream now marks its own transaction
      (SET LOCAL selecto.guard) and checks the marker before rolling back,
      rolls back its savepoint only after its own statement failed, and
      otherwise fails with transaction_state_unsafe, leaving the connection
      untouched for the host. Healthy streams, and a stream's own failures
      (a timeout, a decode error), behave as before.
    - SECURITY/correctness: ending a stream or budget never hides a cleanup
      that left the host's transaction aborted (PostgreSQL's COMMIT of an
      aborted transaction silently rolls back). When a stream's own failure
      cannot be undone, next raises stream_cleanup_failed instead of the
      original error, close raises as before, and an abandoned stream or
      budget whose cleanup failed or was left to the host now warns from
      DESTROY; the aborted transaction, the poisoned handle and the refusal
      of every later guard keep the state detectable.
    - Every Selecto savepoint (writes, bounded write probes, typed insert
      admission, budgets, streams) now has a process-unique name
      (selecto_write_<pid>_<n> instead of selecto_write_<depth>), so a
      cleanup can only ever roll back to its own savepoint. A savepoint
      budget counts as begun only once its savepoint exists.
    - SECURITY: an association source_scope_key that is not a root tenant
      column (for example region_id -> region_id) no longer becomes the
      reference's tenant guard. It was guarded as
      target.region_id IN <tenant set>, which admitted another tenant's row
      in the same region. On a single-column root such a reference is now
      guarded by the target schema's tenant_field and refused with
      invalid_domain (foreign_key_tenant_scope_undeclared) when the target
      declares none; on a two-column root it is refused outright. Both
      refusals happen at engine construction, before any query or write.
    - SECURITY: a declared foreign key and the associations on the same
      owner key must describe one reference. When they name different
      relations, or pair a target tenant column with a different tenant
      column (or a different root tenant column), the domain is refused with
      invalid_domain (code foreign_key_tenant_scope_undeclared) when the
      engine is built and on every write, before any query or DML.
      Previously the declared foreign key silently won and the
      association's tenant guard was skipped, so a write could reference
      another tenant's row. An agreeing foreign key and association are
      guarded once, and an explicit shared foreign key
      (references.tenant_field false) remains usable.
    - BREAKING: public API writes (Selecto::API::EngineHandler) are confined
      to each operation's authored grants. Without an expected_cardinality
      grant an operation permits exactly one row: bulk => 1 is still
      required for update/delete counts above one but no longer admits them
      by itself. Without a returning grant (a field list, true or "all") no
      fields may be returned. Either request is refused with
      invalid_api_write during admission, before any statement runs. Domain
      documents that relied on bulk alone, or on ungranted returning, must
      add expected_cardinality (for example "many" or ["between", 1, 50])
      and returning grants to their writes.operations. Request limits,
      tenant and scope checks, and native/portable write policy are
      unchanged.
    - Selecto::BoundedQuery->prepare_scalar_column joins two prepared bundles
      through the adapter's scalar_column_statement and carries their read
      policy evidence, so a canned page that carries its total on its rows
      still runs on an engine with explicit read tenant policy.
    - Date shortcuts are measured from the database's current date: without
      an explicit today, Selecto::DateShortcut->expression returns the new
      date_shortcut expression, which PostgreSQL and DuckDB compile against
      CURRENT_DATE (or today in the query's use_timezone), so the session
      time zone decides when a day begins. Other adapters still bind the
      server's date. An epoch column now compares as an instant. Shortcut
      bounds are defined once, as terms(), for Perl and SQL alike.
    - Bounded child collections accept a formatted child date
      ({key, expression => datetime_format(...)}); other child expressions
      are still refused.
    - A canned text control with multiple => 1 takes up to 100 values
      separated by whitespace, commas or semicolons (such as pasted VINs)
      and matches a row that starts with any of them.
    - datetime_format takes a timezone option, which shows that one value in
      an IANA zone without localizing the query's filters, and two display
      formats: us_date (10/08/2026) and us_datetime (10/08/2026 2:30 PM),
      on PostgreSQL and DuckDB. A day_minute format (2026-10-08 14:30)
      sorts in date order, so it is also offered for grouping.
    - Canned detail views may select an aliased datetime_format of an
      entity-grain field, and related collections may format a child date
      with {key, expression}. The formatted field joins the detail GROUP BY.
    - Faster API query responses, byte for byte the same. EngineHandler->query
      checks the adapter result (its traversal limits and every cell's
      bytes) and the response's exact JSON bytes
      (Selecto::API::ResponsePolicy->check_json) in one pass each, without a
      method call per cell, and Selecto::API->request skips its second walk
      of the result (public_data's traversal check) and canonical_json's
      per-value validation when the value cannot fail them. Each limit
      compares a running total that only grows, so the single pass decides
      exactly what the previous checks decided; a result or response that
      reaches a limit, or holds anything the single pass does not take
      (blessed or unsupported references, cycles, and in check_json numbers
      that are not integers), goes through the previous checks, which raise
      the same error. Limits, defaults, statuses and response bytes are
      unchanged. 100,000 rows of 10 columns (median of 5 interleaved runs,
      RAM PostgreSQL): EngineHandler->query with a Mojo::JSON encode 4.81 s
      to 1.44 s (arrays) and 6.87 s to 2.09 s (objects); Selecto::API->request
      11.1 s to 2.9 s; peak RSS unchanged. A 50-row objects page: 4.5 to 1.9 ms
      (handler), 9.0 to 3.1 ms (Selecto::API).
    - Breaking: Selecto::PostgreSQL returns the values DBD::Pg fetched,
      untouched, unless canonical values are asked for. Engine::all,
      Engine::stream (bounded too) and Engine::projection_sum take
      canonical_values => 1 per call, and the adapter takes
      canonical_values => 1 (default 0) to make them the default again
      (a call's canonical_values => 0 then asks for driver values). What a
      host that does not ask sees, per PostgreSQL type (DBD::Pg 3): numeric
      keeps its scale ("10.500", not "10.5"); real and
      double precision are Perl numbers, so JSON numbers (1.5, 1e+20),
      instead of their Perl strings ("1.5", "1e+20", "10000000000",
      "Inf"); timestamp and timestamptz keep the server text
      ("2024-01-01 10:00:00+00" or "2024-01-01 04:00:00-06" under the
      session TimeZone, not "2024-01-01T10:00:00"); integers and booleans
      (already numbers and 1/0), text, date, json text and the rest are
      unchanged. To keep the old behaviour:
          Selecto->adapter(postgresql => (dbh => $dbh, canonical_values => 1));
      or $engine->all($query, canonical_values => 1). The API query and
      resource handlers, canned pages, co-domain lookups and export_scalars
      ask for canonical values, so their responses are unchanged. Other
      adapters always return canonical values. A Selecto::PostgreSQL
      subclass that overrides execute_query is not sent the option unless
      its supports('canonical_values') is true.
    - Canonical values are decoded in Perl by default, as before: a host
      that asks for them gets exactly the values 0.2.2 returned, and dates
      and timestamps follow the session DateStyle (a non-ISO DateStyle such
      as SQL, Postgres or German shows through, as it always has). Integer
      columns that DBD::Pg fetched as Perl numbers skip int() on Perl 5.36
      and newer.
    - Opt-in canonical_sql => 1 (default 0) has PostgreSQL format the
      canonical values of fields declared decimal (PostgreSQL 13+), date,
      naive_datetime and utc_datetime in the outermost select list: trim_scale
      for numeric, and TO_CHAR for dates and timestamps, in the session
      TimeZone, with the offset from TO_CHAR's OF unless it is +00. Its
      dates and timestamps are always the ISO form in the session time zone,
      whatever the DateStyle; under ISO they equal the Perl decode (BC,
      infinities, fractions and historic offsets with seconds included). The
      declared type chooses the columns and the column's PostgreSQL type
      (CASE on pg_typeof) the formatter, so a mis-declared temporal kind or a
      date kept as text is unchanged. Columns PostgreSQL does not format
      (integers, floats, booleans, aggregates and other expressions,
      computed fields, ordered rollups, set operations) are decoded in Perl.
      A float or integer column declared decimal comes out as its
      PostgreSQL text ("1e+20", "Infinity"; "7" as a string). ORDER BY and
      GROUP BY keep the underlying expressions. Streams, bounded streams and
      BoundedQuery format the same columns (so a bounded result's cell-size
      guard measures the canonical text). It is off by default because the
      Perl decode measured faster at every result size (100,000 rows: 154 ms
      in Perl, 213 ms in SQL), and PostgreSQL parses and plans the longer
      statement on every call without the statement cache (roughly 30-50
      microseconds per formatted datetime column; point reads were 100-150
      microseconds slower). With canonical_sql off, compile records nothing
      for it.
    - getResource takes an optional date_format query parameter (iso8601,
      the default and the previous output; rfc3339_millis; epoch_seconds;
      epoch_milliseconds) that formats every temporal field in UTC,
      compiled into the SQL through datetime_format. Under the non-default
      formats a date is midnight UTC and a naive datetime is UTC wall time;
      epochs round down; null stays null. Any other value is
      invalid_api_query with details {parameter => 'date_format'} (public
      error details may now carry parameter). Documented in the OpenAPI
      document (an enum), Selecto::API and Selecto::API::EngineHandler;
      EngineHandler->resource_date_formats lists the values.
    - Govern individual protected SQLite UPSERTs against their actual native
      conflict branch, exact update mask, literal defaults and transaction-bound
      private receipts. Compile empty masks as DO NOTHING while retaining caller
      cardinality. Resolve choices from the final canonical persisted candidate.
      Refuse UPSERTs whose TRANSACTION or EVIDENCE normalizers would change a
      persisted value, since their rules would test a value that is never stored.
    - Add bounded value.compare_path rules with absolute root dependencies,
      exact related-value comparisons and unconditional resource preflight.
    - Preserve native integer, unsigned integer, floating and authored text
      identity through bounded rule/policy copies, including canonical domain
      parsing and returned subjects. Admit literal opening brackets in bounded
      ASCII character classes while refusing POSIX/intersection syntax.
    - Preserve rule, validator and action scalar identity through domain
      composition, overlay DSL construction and action planning/serialization,
      including submitted JSON and declared action literals/defaults.
    - Compile sealed portable operations through trusted tenant/context fills,
      native foreign-key guards, required/captured query predicates and SQLite
      typed insert admission. Preserve nested generated-key ownership and named
      atomic batches across explicitly configured domains. Keep internal graph
      identity keys separate from public returning ceilings. Refuse authored
      document action policies until their full native mutation path exists.
    - Verify the complete six portable write models through production APIs,
      and expose genuine private planned-operation authorization/execution.
    - Validate the complete bounded portable write contract and expose sealed
      authored operation snapshots. Keep declarations separate from grants.
    - Preflight versioned native write capabilities before dispatch, preserving
      requested returning, generated bindings, transactions and atomic graphs.
      Exercise the complete 30-state capability safety model.
    - Enforce explicit returning/cardinality limits, required insert fields,
      protected upsert fields and atomic write-once guards. Check cardinality
      ranges before commit and retain caller-owned transaction/savepoint work.
    - Add complete bounded choice-source metadata validation and public choice
      binding/request/options APIs with explicit trusted host providers and
      sealed native membership/options results. Preserve actual join target
      authority, full aliases and unique established nested shorthand.
    - Add bounded plain-domain schema validation for relation metadata, query
      libraries, event payload schemas and consumer registry envelopes. Cover
      imported native required filters when caller queries add disjunctions.
    - Add public metadata domain identity APIs and native identity observations.
    - Publish stable provider contract snapshots and verify consumer fields,
      filters, query members, versions, fingerprints, and required scopes.
      Snapshot diffs distinguish additive surfaces from breaking removals,
      result type changes, and added required scopes. A public exhaustive
      32-state compatibility model checks two independent invariants.
    - Implement the complete 27-operator DataRules v1 AST and six text
      normalizers with exact arithmetic, bounded ASCII patterns, shared
      resource budgets and unconditional advisory/condition resource errors.
    - Preserve submitted INPUT values separately from physical SQLite
      candidates; consumed generated fields remain pending across logical
      branches and conditions. Batch inserts preflight all rules before DML.
    - Prove native graph/owned-set ownership with actual key affinities,
      collations, complete single-key uniqueness and every present FK group.
      Protected candidate storage refuses generated columns and automatic
      foreign-key updates outside the admitted plan.
    - Govern native SQLite stored candidates and complete child collections inside
      their write transaction, applying required rules and normalizers before writes.
    - Execute owned one-root action collection patches with policy-scoped read
      anchors, exact returned client identities, plan revalidation, and atomic rollback.
    - SQLite write graphs support explicitly declared owned-set reconciliation.
      Cleanup uses actual RETURNING identities, whole composite keys and
      independently derived parent, child tenant and required scopes. Empty
      sets, exact generated identities, duplicate refusal and late rollback
      are verified with native in-memory SQLite. Canonical required_filters
      now guard both reads and writes, including nested domains.
    - SQLite supports typed values, JSON extraction and recursive typed JSON
      containment, JSON/array rowsets, array predicates, UTC calendar formats
      and buckets, FTS5 predicates/ranking, schema metadata and query plans.
      Version gates compare full tuples; CTEs require 3.8.3, windows 3.28.0,
      and bounded materialization/RETURNING 3.35.0. Offset-only paging uses
      LIMIT -1. Governed query/stream execution rejects writes and preserves
      caller-owned query_only and raw/DBI transactions; trusted tooling has
      an explicit execute_query_unsafe escape.
    - SQLite ordered/bounded related collections retain boolean/JSON types,
      child/bridge policies and tenant guards. Limits/cursors and API subtables
      execute with stable ordering and whole-response overflow refusal.
    - Canonical direct associations infer tenant equality from both declared
      relation tenant fields, including nested edges, correlated collections,
      computed EXISTS and PostgreSQL lateral lookups. Explicit complete pairs
      retain precedence; either partial pair is invalid_domain. Tenantless
      targets, legacy explicit scope and through contracts retain their policy.
    - Explicit hidden column metadata now withholds the field from every
      public surface using Domain::field_is_public, matching internal and
      redacted fields. API query roles and named routes refuse hidden_field
      (403), while trusted Engine reads remain available.
    - Public API queries enforce explicit filterable/sortable field roles on
      direct intent and named query-library routes, with invalid_field (422).
      Optional bounded group_by enforces public/groupable fields and refuses
      nongrouped selections or orderings before database execution.
    - Public API query pages publish has_more from one extra fetched row and
      return at most the requested limit. Zero-sized pages fetch no rows.
    - Opt-in PostgreSQL statement cache. Selecto::PostgreSQL takes
      statement_cache (default 0) and statement_cache_size (default 256).
      When on, execute_query and executed writes reuse one statement handle
      per SQL text and connection (kept with prepare_cached under a private
      attribute, so it is freed with the handle), and DBD::Pg runs them as
      named server-side statements. Least recently used handles beyond the
      bound are deallocated, only right after a statement succeeded. A lost
      statement (26000) or changed result type (0A000) is prepared again
      once outside a transaction; inside one the original error and
      SQLSTATE stand. New Selecto::PostgreSQL::StatementCache (forget,
      count). Unchanged with it off (the default): every call prepares a new
      handle as before. Streams, insert admission and bounded-write probes
      always prepare afresh. Not for transaction-mode poolers before
      PgBouncer 1.21.
    - Each domain action with preconditions or a transition gets a boolean
      can_<action> column, true when the row meets them, with Yes/No filter
      choices, so the API and Explorer can show and filter the rows an
      action could run on. Actions opt out with prerequisite_column false.
      Domain::without_action_prerequisites removes the columns of actions a
      caller cannot see, and Selecto::Action->prerequisites returns the
      guards without planning a target.
    - Selecto::API serves GET .../resources/{id} (getResource) when constructed
      with resources => 1; a resource's aggregate_version is its ETag, and
      resource_not_found maps to 404.
    - Selecto::API::EngineHandler reads one resource by primary key with
      resource(), and publishes a virtual, read-only aggregate_version field
      through an optional host versioner. Resources and queries return it only
      when it is requested.
    - API subtables may be ordered by an internal child primary key. The key
      only orders the bounded collection and is never returned.
    - Trusted adapters may admit native flat array parameters under finite
      bind-slot, element and conservative wire-byte budgets. Other adapters
      retain scalar-only parameter admission.
    - Query compilation admits Selecto::Query subclasses used by typed SQL
      pattern builders while retaining depth, size, cycle and reference checks.
    - Native adapters without a DBI handle keep normalized query_error
      responses when their state is locked. SQLSTATE inspection now checks
      whether a handle exists before reading it.
    - Database failures keep the driver's SQLSTATE. A query_error from
      normalize_error now carries details.sqlstate and details.category
      (unique_violation, foreign_key_violation, not_null_violation,
      check_violation, query_canceled, serialization_failure,
      deadlock_detected, or database_error), as in the Elixir library, so a
      host can tell a lock timeout (55P03), deadlock (40P01) or
      serialization failure (40001) apart and retry. Before, the code was
      dropped, and the write's rollback cleared it from the handle. Insert
      admission and Selecto::Stream now normalize before finishing or
      closing the statement, which also cleared it. New
      Selecto::Adapter->driver_sqlstate. Unchanged: the code (query_error),
      the message, details.cause, the specific constraint errors, and API
      responses, whose details allow-list leaves sqlstate out outside debug.
    - A missing tenant scope is refused with 403 through Selecto::API, as
      in the other runtimes. Selecto::API::error_status now returns 403 for
      missing_tenant_scope (was 422), so Selecto::API->request answers a
      handler error with that code and no status with 403, as does the
      documented host guard that passes status => error_status($code).
      Changed responses: EngineHandler->query or ->write on a tenant_field
      domain whose engine has no trusted tenant boundary (and any other
      missing_tenant_scope a handler returns without a status) now answer
      403 instead of 422. Unchanged: the code, the body (details {} and the
      redacted message outside debug_sql), a status the handler sets, the
      Selecto::Error thrown by the engine and handler, every other code.
    - Queries that name a hidden field are refused as hidden_field (403
      through Selecto::API), as in the other runtimes. Changed responses,
      all from EngineHandler->query: a select entry (plain, object or
      subtable), projection, view, segment, view segment, named ordering,
      view ordering, order_by or filter that reaches an internal or
      redact_fields field used to fail with field_not_public and now fails
      with hidden_field (details {field}; message "Field is not exposed.");
      through Selecto::API a handler error without a status now answers
      hidden_field with 403 instead of 422 (new Selecto::API::error_status).
      Within select, an unknown field or another invalid entry is now
      reported before a hidden one, and no type or association check runs
      on a hidden field (a date format on a hidden text field is now
      hidden_field, not invalid_api_query). Unchanged: unknown fields
      (unknown_field), write assignments, write filters and returning
      fields (field_not_public), conditional choice filters reading
      internal fields (field_not_public), every other code and status.
    - CSV and TSV exports follow the certified cross-runtime cell rules
      (api_export_rules) instead of Text::CSV's defaults. Changed export
      bytes: a cell is no longer quoted for a space alone ("two words",
      " ", "trail "); a cell with any non-ASCII character is now always
      quoted (before, only when its UTF-8 bytes included 0x7F-0xA0, so
      "café"/"Renée" were bare while "東京" was quoted); control characters
      (0x01-0x08, 0x0B, 0x0C, 0x0E-0x1F, 0x7F) no longer force quotes, and a
      NUL is written as is instead of the escaped "0; a formula lead after
      leading Unicode White_Space ("  =1+1", "\x{A0}=x", VT/FF then "=")
      is now neutralized with a leading "'". Headers follow the same rules.
      Unchanged: separators, quotes, tab/CR/LF, leading =,+,-,@ and
      tab/CR/LF, nulls, booleans, numbers, nested JSON, CRLF row endings.
    - Opt-in export scalars: Engine->all($query, export_scalars => 1) and
      EngineHandler->query($engine, $body, export_scalars => 1) return
      NUMERIC as PostgreSQL's text at the column scale ("533.10",
      "7152.00", "0.0000"), booleans as JSON booleans and json/jsonb as
      decoded values, for exports. Only the PostgreSQL adapter supports it;
      other adapters refuse it (unsupported_feature). Without the option,
      results are unchanged.
    - Faster large IN lists with the same SQL, parameters, limits and
      refusals: operation budgets admit a list of values in one pass (each
      value still checked in order, each maximum looked up once), recognise
      core booleans without a JSON::PP::is_bool call per scalar, and count
      bytes without a bytes::length call; association discovery no longer
      walks plain IN members. A 500-value IN filter now composes and
      compiles in about 1.7 ms instead of 3.4 ms locally.
    - Faster query composition, compilation and write governance with the
      same resource limits, thresholds and error codes: inlined operation
      budget comparisons (the breach path is unchanged), one admission per
      query under Engine->compile instead of two, a single deep copy in write
      command derivation, no whole-contract copy to read one section, no
      copies of field metadata or the applied query library for internal
      reads, and a shared default limits policy.
    - Faster bounded results: Selecto::BoundedQuery (canned pages, component
      result pages) fetches min(max_rows + 1, 100) guarded rows per round
      trip instead of one, and QueryBudget->check(defer_rearm => 1) lets a
      bounded stream refresh the server statement timeout right before each
      FETCH instead of once per row. The wall deadline is still checked on
      every row and the server deadline before every fetch. 500 guarded rows
      locally: 64 ms -> 25 ms; a 50-row page 8.1 ms -> 4.4 ms.
    - Bounded PostgreSQL streams fetch fetch_size rows per round trip
      (default 1, as before) instead of always one, read column types once
      and decode each batch column by column. Every existing bounded caller
      passes fetch_size => 1, so nothing changes until a caller asks for more;
      docs/security-boundaries.md describes the new bound.
    - Faster query results: execute_query fetches with fetchall_arrayref and
      PostgreSQL decodes column by column, choosing each column's type branch
      once instead of per cell. Results are identical (t/decode_rows.t checks
      every cell against _decode). On the performance lane's 5,000 x 20
      result this halves query time (70 ms to 34 ms). Adapters with their own
      _decode keep the per-cell path; one whose _decode is the identity skips
      decoding.
    - Expression->from_filter_ast reads a decoded JSON boolean comparison value
      or in member as the literal 1 or 0 instead of refusing it as a reference.
    - 0.2.2 security remediation: bound complete filter/value-expression trees,
      scalar and whole-operation parameter bytes, exact rule numeric work,
      non-backtracking ASCII patterns, and importer preview amplification.
    - Refuse unverifiable typed governed inserts before DML; use verified
      SQLite/PostgreSQL storage types, rounding and collations. Fix computed
      IN operand binding order. See docs/typed-insert-admission.md.
    - Bound ordinary UI/canned result consumption and final API JSON/CSV/TSV/
      XLSX bytes. SQL diagnostics and full-domain publication now default off;
      trusted debug/publication configuration is required to enable them.
    - Security: apply trusted finite budgets to query-library expansion, CSV
      inspection, action targets, and nested public API results. Oversized
      nested collections are refused before returning partial data.
    - Security: remove hidden field values and matched-row error counts;
      enforce public co-domain lookup fields and authority-specific file hold
      release with audit-before-mutation behavior.
    - Security: preserve selected action upsert identity, require declared
      conflict targets on all governed paths, and refuse MySQL/MariaDB upserts
      whose requested identity cannot be guaranteed. Separate authored
      system-now instructions from caller literals.
    - Security: preserve SELECT/JOIN positional parameter order, require
      concrete public non-bulk write targets, and bound broad PostgreSQL/SQLite
      writes before DML. Other adapters refuse unsupported broad writes.
    - Add scoped database execution budgets and verified bounded PostgreSQL
      cursor / SQLite streaming for consumers. See docs/security-boundaries.md
      for migration requirements, defaults and evidence limits.
    - A joins entry that is not an object is refused with invalid_domain
      ("join <name> must be an object") instead of being silently ignored,
      as the Ruby, .NET and Python cores already do.
    - Security: the permissive write policy is removed. Strict is the only
      behaviour: an engine refuses (write_policy_missing) a write to a
      domain without writes.operations, or without writes.fields for
      anything but a delete, and a graph edge without its relationship
      domain. Selecto::Engine->new and from_registry still accept
      write_policy => 'strict', but any other value, including
      'permissive', throws invalid_write_policy. Previously
      write_policy => 'permissive' let a domain without a writes section be
      written. Code that relied on it must declare a writes contract.
    - Selecto::Query->retarget($path, strategy => 'in' | 'exists') moves a
      query's row grain from the domain root to the relation at an
      association path. Earlier filters become the context: the result is
      the distinct target rows the root query's joined read reaches under
      them and the domain's required predicate. Later filters, grouping,
      ordering, and pagination apply to the target. A tenant-scoped target
      carries the root tenant condition or fails closed.
    - The domain retarget section may declare targets (with label and
      default_selected) and a default_target; declared targets form an
      allow-list. Selecto::Retarget exposes the target and relation domains.
    - Security: Selecto::CannedPage->new refuses (invalid_canned_page) a
      definition that names a field the domain withholds from public
      surfaces (redact_fields at any level, or an internal column; see
      Domain::field_is_public) anywhere: the entity key, the dataset
      predicate, view selections (aliases, expressions and collection
      children, filters and orderings), groups and orderings, and control
      fields and label fields. The message names the part and the field
      ("view list references redacted field secret_token") and details
      carry {field, view | control | dataset}. Previously such a page ran
      and showed the field.
    - Security: Selecto::CannedPage->run asserts the tenant boundary for
      every engine, not only a Selecto::Engine. An engine without
      assert_tenant_boundary is refused (missing_tenant_scope) on a
      tenant_field domain; previously a delegating engine skipped the check
      and read every tenant.
    - Security: action grants expire 300 seconds after grant_action unless
      expires_in says otherwise, and expires_in may not exceed 3600
      (invalid_action_grant). Previously a grant without expires_in never
      expired.
    - Security: the API query handler refuses (field_not_public) a
      requested or view segment that reads an internal or redacted field and
      a named ordering that sorts by one. A components.filter_choices
      conditional filter whose fields are not all public accepts only its
      declared choice values and no null test.
    - Security: an update may not set the tenant field to a tenant the
      domain's required tenant predicate excludes, or to a computed value
      (tenant_mismatch); previously it could move a row to another tenant
      when the field was updatable.
    - Security: MySQL and MariaDB refuse tenant-scoped upserts
      (unsupported_scope_predicate): ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE fires on any
      unique key, so a primary-key collision could update another tenant's
      row.
    - SQL Server escapes [ in starts_with, text_contains and ends_with
      patterns, so it no longer opens a character class.
    - Expression->from_filter_ast refuses filters nested more than 64 levels
      deep (invalid_query).
    - Read compiles are linear in the size of an expression: grouped
      expressions are matched through memoized structural digests, and not
      looked up at all when a query has none. A 7,500-deep predicate took
      about 110 seconds to compile and now takes under 0.1.
    - Selecto::Expression->field_references (formerly private to
      CannedPage) lists every field path an expression reads.
    - Security (TW-05): a write with trusted tenants (the engine tenant or
      a required tenant predicate) may only reference a parent row of one of
      those tenants. References are writes.constraints.foreign_keys
      (references.tenant_field names the parent's tenant column, or false for
      a shared relation) and the owner keys of direct to-one associations.
      Each assigned reference becomes an EXISTS guard in the write statement
      (insert and upsert through SELECT ... WHERE, update in its WHERE, both
      MERGE branches on SQL Server); a refused write fails with
      cardinality_mismatch even without expected_count, the same as a
      missing parent. A reference whose tenancy the domain does not record
      fails closed on a tenant-scoped write (invalid_domain,
      foreign_key_tenant_scope_undeclared). Write::Command carries the
      guards (foreign_key_guards); the engine sets them on every governed
      command.

0.2.0  2026-09-30
    - First CPAN release: governed domains, queries, portable writes,
      governed actions, and database adapters for PostgreSQL, MySQL,
      MariaDB, SQL Server, SQLite, and DuckDB.
    - Licensed under the Artistic License 2.0.
    - utc_datetime columns accept "storage": "naive_utc" for zone-less
      timestamps that hold UTC (Rails datetime columns). On PostgreSQL and
      DuckDB such a column is read as (column AT TIME ZONE 'UTC') wherever
      it is used as an instant: under use_timezone and in the iso8601,
      rfc3339_millis, epoch and timezone_offset formats. Other adapters
      accept the hint and compile as before. Domain::field_storage reports
      it.
    - Managed writes no longer commit or roll back a transaction the host
      opened. When the DBI handle is inside a transaction (AutoCommit off
      after begin_work, an AutoCommit => 0 handle, or a raw BEGIN the
      server reports on PostgreSQL, MySQL/MariaDB or SQL Server) a write,
      batch or graph runs in a savepoint (SAVEPOINT selecto_write_N /
      RELEASE, ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT on failure; SAVE TRANSACTION on SQL
      Server): it commits or rolls back with the host, a failed write
      undoes only itself, and a PostgreSQL transaction stays usable. The
      host now commits an AutoCommit => 0 handle itself. DuckDB has no
      savepoints and refuses such a write with invalid_adapter. A
      transaction that failed to begin is no longer rolled back, begin,
      commit and savepoint failures are Selecto errors, and DuckDB now
      honours transaction_mode => 'external'.
    - SQL adapters accept transaction_handler, a code reference that runs
      each managed write in the host's own transaction API.
    - Every required predicate now guards engine writes, not only reads.
      This deliberately goes beyond the shared protocol's earlier rule
      that required predicates are read scopes (previously only a
      tenant_field domain without writes.scope.tenant applied its required
      predicate to writes). A predicate over root fields is ANDed into
      every update and delete, so they match only rows inside it and
      expected_count counts only those rows; inserted rows must satisfy it
      (query_rule_violation, or query_rule_not_evaluable when a value it
      reads is not assigned); and upsert is refused with
      query_enforcement_unsupported_operation ("upsert is not supported on
      a domain with a required predicate"). A predicate that reads any
      association field refuses every write on the domain with
      query_rule_unsupported_field ("association fields are not portable
      write guards", details relation and the sorted association field
      paths); reads
      are unaffected. writes.scope.tenant still applies and both must
      hold. This covers execute_write, preview_write, write_command,
      execute_batch, the root of execute_graph, preview_action and
      execute_action. The new Engine::required_write_guard exposes the
      check, and Selecto::API::EngineHandler now defers to it: its upsert
      refusal uses the engine message, and write_command no longer puts
      the required predicate on the returned command, so the engine
      applies it exactly once.
